diagnostic_evidence.quote:
Each time step of UM-SRD can be decomposed into:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Pre-merge: apply $S_j$ to map $U^n$ to neighborhood averages $Q^{n,-1}$ (lifted to the original mesh). By Proposition~\ref{prop:TVD-SRD},
  \begin{equation}
    TV(Q^{n,-1}) \le TV(U^n).
  \end{equation}
  \item Neighborhood finite volume update on the merged mesh: $Q^{n,-1} \mapsto Q^n$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:TVD-Q-update},
  \begin{equation}
    TV(Q^n) \le TV(Q^{n,-1}).
  \end{equation}
  \item Blended redistribution: $Q^n \mapsto U^{n+1}$ via $R_j(s_j)$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:TVD-R},
  \begin{equation}
    TV(U^{n+1}) \le TV(Q^n).
  \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

In component form, for $i \in M_j$,
\begin{equation}
  U^{n+1}_i = (1 - s_j) U^*_i + s_j \sum_{k \in W_i} w_{i,k} \hat{Q}^*_k,
\end{equation}
while for cells $i$ not in any small-cell neighborhood we set $U^{n+1}_i = U^*_i$.

failure_location.quote:
Under the assumptions above (1D, one small cell with $\alpha < 1/2$, merging left, first-order upwind, CFL $a \Delta t / h \le 1$), the UM-SRD time-stepping operator is TVD on the original mesh:
\begin{equation}
  TV(U^{n+1}) \le TV(U^n) \quad \text{for all } n \ge 0.
\end{equation}
